xping for Debian / Ubuntu
-------------------------

Shell completion
  Tab completion for bash, zsh and fish is installed with this package
  and works in new shells without any setup:
    bash  /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/xping
    zsh   /usr/share/zsh/vendor-completions/_xping
    fish  /usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d/xping.fish
  bash needs the bash-completion package (installed by default on Ubuntu).
  You do not need "xping completion --install" with this package.

ICMP without root
  ping, trace, mtr and health use unprivileged ICMP ("ping") sockets,
  which Ubuntu allows by default (net.ipv4.ping_group_range). If your
  system restricts them, xping falls back to the ping/traceroute
  binaries (package Recommends: iputils-ping, traceroute). "xping deps"
  shows which mode is in use.

Files (all in your home directory, none are created until used)
  ~/.xping/checks.toml          default checks of "xping check" and
                                "xping monitor" (xping check --init)
  ~/.xping/config.toml          personal defaults (xping config)
  ~/.xping/history/             results kept with --save
  ~/.xping/monitor.log          output of the background monitor
  ~/.xping/profiles.json        saved profiles (xping profile add ...)

Background monitor
  "xping monitor --install-service" runs the monitor as a systemd user
  unit (~/.config/systemd/user/xping-monitor.service). It starts at
  login; to keep it running while you are logged out:
    loginctl enable-linger $USER
  Remove it with "xping monitor --uninstall-service".

Documentation
  man xping, and https://mehaskari.github.io/xping/guide/

